# Modular Service / Repository Pattern

## Layer duties

| Layer | Responsibility | Must not |
|---|---|---|
| Controller | Authorize → call service → return Inertia/JSON | Business rules, Eloquent queries, validation rules |
| Form Request | Input validation & authorization hooks | Persist data, call AI/providers |
| Service | Business logic, domain exceptions, orchestration | Raw HTTP/Inertia response shaping beyond DTOs |
| Repository | Eloquent queries, always `user_id`-scoped | Business policy (depth limits, “folder not empty”) |
| Policy | Ownership / ability checks | Queries beyond simple ownership |

## Thin controller example

```php
// ✅ GOOD
public function store(CreateAssetRequest $request): RedirectResponse
{
    $asset = $this->assetService->create($request->user(), $request->validated());

    return redirect()->back();
}

// ❌ BAD — logic + Eloquent in controller
public function store(Request $request)
{
    $asset = Asset::create([...$request->all(), 'user_id' => auth()->id()]);
    if ($asset->folder && $asset->folder->depth > 2) { /* ... */ }
}
```

## Module layout

```
app/Modules/{Name}/
  Controllers/  Requests/  Services/  Repositories/
  Models/  Policies/  routes.php
```

Bind `*RepositoryInterface` → `*Repository` in `ModuleServiceProvider`.
Inject interfaces into services, not concrete repositories.
